+### 9. State the evaluation identity once per page
+
+The human page names the evaluation on every claim's own element. The
+evaluation identity is one value per page, and as measured on 2026-09-13
+(`docs/evidence/pwb-m1-polaris-lane-a-measurement-2026-09-13.json`) it was
+repeated on 713 claim tuples with one distinct value; the page after the lane A
+trim was 1,478,637 bytes direct and 1,484,487 bytes through the tailnet mount,
+above the 1,400,000-byte working target ruled in P-67 and under the 2 MiB
+response ceiling. The amendment lets that one value be stated once on a
+page-level scope (PWB-REQ-007), valid only when every claim has it in the
+machine answer; where a claim differs, each claim carries its own and nothing
+fails to render. The scope states the value as text on its own element, read
+before the claims, so the non-visual reading (PWB-REQ-016, RFC7-34) meets it
+once where the sighted reading does. The machine answer never inherits and
+keeps the value on every claim. Every oracle expands the scope with its own
+statement of the rule, so the comparator still imports no rendering code, and
+PWB-REQ-020 gains the `stamp-hidden` falsifier and mutant class beside its
+unchanged `collapsed` and `duplicated` falsifiers. Rejected: scoping a label,
+tier, reason, freshness, challenge or any other epistemic field, because a
+claim reached apart from its scope would then lack a VIS-1/VIS-2 distinction
+RFC7-33 requires on the unit; scoping the `non-citable` /
+`presentation-artifact` pair, which RFC7-33's sub-clause requires on every
+unit; an inheritance rule known only to the renderer.
